      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "First, we need to find out how many miles Ryosuke drove his friend back home from work. We can do this by subtracting the initial odometer reading from the final odometer reading:\n\nFinal odometer reading - Initial odometer reading = Distance driven\n74,592 - 74,568 = 24 miles\n\nNow we know that Ryosuke drove his friend 24 miles to get home.\n\nSince Ryosuke's car gets 28 miles per gallon, we can calculate how many gallons of gas he used by dividing the distance driven by the car's fuel efficiency:\n\nDistance driven / Car's fuel efficiency = Gallons of gas used\n24 miles / 28 miles per gallon = 0.857 gallons (rounded to 3 decimal places)\n\nNow we know that Ryosuke used 0.857 gallons of gas to drive his friend back home.\n\nWe also know that one gallon of gas costs $4.05. So to find the total cost of the gas, we multiply the number of gallons used by the price per gallon:\n\nCost of gas = Gallons of gas used * Price per gallon\nCost of gas = 0.857 gallons * $4.05\nCost of gas = $3.51635 (rounded to 5 decimal places)\n\nRounding to the nearest cent, the total cost of the gas used is $3.52.",
